/P 475 0 R
/E (Preventing unsafe sleep is an effective way to improve infant health outcomes when considering leading and preventable causes of death. Sleep-related deaths were a quarter of TN infant deaths in 2023, and of those, nearly 80% were likely preventable.18 While county-specific data was not available, the Child Fatality Review Team for Rutherford County highlighted unsafe sleep prevention education methods as a primary opportunity for improvement.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10 1247 0 R 11]
>>
endobj
790 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [12]
/Pg 18 0 R
/P 1247 0 R
>>
endobj
791 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 475 0 R
/E (The 2023 TDH Infant Mortality Report highlights eight common risk factors in sleep-related death case narratives. The most common three factors were Unsafe Sleep Practices \(77% of  deaths\); Objects in Bed \(49%\), and Substance Use/Exposure \(32%\). One of the least common factors was being Uneducated on Safe Sleep \(occured in 2% of deaths\).18 )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [13 1248 0 R 14]
>>
endobj
792 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [15]
/Pg 18 0 R
/P 1248 0 R
>>
endobj
793 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 475 0 R
/E (This may support that the need is more nuanced than education alone. Merging standard education resources alongside emerging best practice, motivational interviewing, culturally-responsive methods, and community-based interventions may lead to greater community application and adherence to safe sleep recommendations \(Appendix D\). )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
