985 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 482 0 R
/T (Considering various cultural norms is crucial when designing safe infant sleep promotion programs.    )
/E (Considering various cultural norms is crucial when designing safe infant sleep promotion programs.    )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
986 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 482 0 R
/E (When implementing safe infant sleep promotion programs, it is important to consider cultural aspects. Vilvens, Vaughn, Southworth, et al. emphasize that health educators should tailor messages to meet individual's needs, which includes incorporating cultural and family advice for childbearing. This approach ensures that the information resonates with the target audience and is more likely to be accepted. 26 Furthermore, this claim is supported by Ball and Volpe, who discussed the importance of confronting and modifying cultural traditions in their article. They noted that for many groups of parents, bed sharing is part of their cultural identity. To ensure effectiveness of interventions, researchers and health educators must recognize the role of bed-sharing in some cultures and families. By acknowledging these cultural practices, educators can develop more culturally-sensitive and effective safe sleep promotion programs. 30 )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3 6 4 7 5 8]
>>
endobj
987 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 482 0 R
/T (Leveraging community voices to disseminate information about safe sleep practices enhances the effectiveness and reach of educational initiatives.   )
/E (Leveraging community voices to disseminate information about safe sleep practices enhances the effectiveness and reach of educational initiatives.   )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
988 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 482 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10 13 11 14 12 15]
>>
endobj
989 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 482 0 R
/T (Conclusion    )
/E (Conclusion    )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
990 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 482 0 R
/E (This literature review has explored several critical factors that influence the effectiveness of safe sleep education for caregivers and adherence to safe sleep practice. The evidence supports the following key recommendations:  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
991 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2524 0 R
/E (Healthcare providers prioritizing establishing strong, trusting relationships with caregivers, and providing safe sleep education continually over time.  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
992 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2525 0 R
/E (Incorporating model/practice safe sleep spaces in safe sleep health education programs/curriculums.  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
993 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2526 0 R
/E (Considering various cultural norms and traditions when designing safe-sleep programs.  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
994 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2527 0 R
/E (Leveraging community advisors to share accurate safe sleep practices and information within their own communities.  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
995 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 482 0 R
/E (The synthesis of these claims highlights the multifaceted approach needed to improve infant sleep effectively. By fostering trust between healthcare providers and caregivers, incorporating comprehensive education and safe sleep environments, considering various cultural norms, and leveraging community voices, public health initiatives can achieve significant improvements in infant mortality outcomes. Future research should continue to explore strategies and interventions that address these critical areas.  )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
